RESULT OF THIS AUDIT AND ITS LIMIT
----------------------------------
One unmarked stale line found and fixed (the handoff's closing "the scope limits and this handoff are 23-F112/F114",
which named withdrawn rows as current). All other summary documents clean on the phrases checked.

LIMIT, and it is the point: **this audit checks PHRASES I thought of, not contradictions in general.** It would not catch
a summary that is stale in wording I did not anticipate — which is exactly how the FINAL_CHAIN_LINE staleness arose (the
phrase "NOT identified" was caught only on a manual read). A phrase list is a spot check, not an audit; the only reliable
check is reading each summary against the findings table, which is what the rule page says about prose documents
accumulating defects the way code does.

OPERATIONAL HALF, added after three instances in one hour taught the same lesson
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A phrase search finds **the wording you changed**, not the sentences that **depend on the thing you changed**. All three
of tonight's misses were of that kind: F133 kept "the root's FIDE identity" after the claim moved to "the searched
position"; job60's RESULT kept the old form; the handoff's body kept telling the old story under a STOP notice. None
contained the phrase I grepped for.

So the procedure is not "grep for the old phrasing". It is:

1. write down the claim that changed, in one sentence;
2. list every OTHER sentence whose truth depends on it — by reading the documents that summarise it, not by searching;
3. read those sentences against the new claim.

That is slower than a grep and it is the only version that works, because dependency is a semantic relation and a phrase
list is a lexical one.

AND A DOCUMENT SUPERSEDED MUST BE REWRITTEN, NOT CAPTIONED. The handoff had a STOP notice at the top and the old,
coherent-but-false account beneath it. A reader who skips the header reads a false document; a reader who reads the header
and then the body gets two contradictory accounts and has to adjudicate. A caption cannot fix a body: if the body is
wrong, the body changes.
